  "query_preview": "..\n/Issued 519 AM MDT Tue Jul 28 2026/ \n\n - Meteorological Overview:\n\nUpper level ridging will dominate through much of the forecast \nperiod, with the ridge bringing continued hot temperatures to most \nlocations from Southwest through North Central Montana until \nSaturday. By Sunday a potent longwave trough will breakdown the \nridge and bring increasing winds and slightly cooler temperatures to \nthe Northern Rockies, with temperatures falling below normal into \nthe day on Monday in wake of a cold front. Monsoonal moisture \nlifting up and around the ridge will provide daily chances for \nisolated showers and storms; however, most locations will remain dry \nover the next week. - Moldan\n\n - Forecast Confidence & Scenarios:\n\nReturn of Extreme Temperatures by Saturday...\n\nWhile temperatures will remain hot through the remainder of the work \nweek climate anomaly indicators have honed in on Saturday being the \nhottest day of the upcoming week, with values becoming unusual to \nvery unusual with respect to late July/early August climatology. The \nfact that these climate anomaly indicators are suggesting another \nperiod of hot temperatures is remarkable considering that August 1st \nis (at most locations) only one day past the climatological peak for \naverage high temperatures across Southwest through North Central \nMontana. The table below represents the potential high temperature \nscenarios for select cities across Southwest through North Central \nMontana, with the \"Low End\" scenario being the NBM10Pct (10% chance \nthat temperatures are lower than the listed value), \"Most Likely\" \nscenario being the NBM50Pct, and the \"High End\" scenario being the \nNBM90Pct (10% chance that temperatures exceed the listed value).\n\n                    Saturday's High Temperature Scenarios\nLOCATION               Low End | Most Likely | High End\nBrowning                  88   |      93     |     98\nCut Bank                  95   |     100     |    104\nHavre                    103   |     108     |    111\nGreat Falls              102   |     105     |    107\nLewistown                 98   |     101     |    104\nHelena                    97   |     100     |    104\nWhite Sulphur Springs     95   |     102     |    101       \nBozeman                   98   |      98     |    105\nDillon                    95   |      98     |    100\nEnnis                     94   |      98     |    100\nWest Yellowstone          89   |      93     |     96\n\nGiven the presence of regional wildfires across the Northern Rockies \nthe \"High End\" scenario will be tough to reach due to decreased \nsolar insolation from the smoke, with dense smoke having the ability \nto hold temperatures back some 5 to 10 degrees.\n\nStrong Winds from Saturday night through Sunday...\n\nConfidence is increasing that a period of strong, nearing high, \nwinds will impact the Rocky Mountain Front from Saturday night \nthrough the day on Sunday due to the combination of mountain wave \nactivity (from the late evening hours on Saturday through Sunday \nmorning) and deep mixing (during the daylight hourS on Sunday). H700 \nwinds per NAEFS analysis are prog'd to reach 40-50kts from Saturday \nevening through Sunday morning across the aforementioned area, which \nis 2 to nearly 3 standard deviations above normal. The table below \nare the NBM5.0 probabilities for wind gusts to exceed 45 mph, 55 \nmph, and 65 mph for select locations along the Rocky Mountain Front \nfor the previously mentioned timeframe.\n\n                    Probabilities for Wind Gusts to Exceed\nLOCATION               >45 mph |   >55 mph   |  >65 mph\nMany Glacier Valley      55%   |     15%     |    <5%\nLogan Pass               50%   |     10%     |     0%\nSt Mary                  80%   |     40%     |    10%\nTwo Medicine Valley      70%   |     30%     |    10%\nEast Glacier             75%   |     25%     |    10%\nBrowning                 70%   |     25%     |     5%\nCut Bank                 75%   |     20%     |     5%\nHeart Butte              80%   |     30%     |    20%\nChoteau                   5%   |      0%     |     0%       \nAugusta                  20%   |     <5%     |     0%\n\nThose looking to travel or recreate along the Rocky Mountain Front \nshould be prepared for strong winds; which could make driving and \nhiking difficult, lead to falling trees in the backcountry, and/or \nlead to the spread of existing/new wildfires. - Moldan",
